---
/**
 * Hero and the single line of framing that precedes the questions.
 *
 * qs-2.0 deleted the scope panel: a six-row table explaining duration,
 * what you receive, what is assessed, what is not assessed, contact
 * details and answer handling, followed by a transmission statement and
 * a processing notice. Around two hundred words of explanation before a
 * single question, written to survive a review rather than to make
 * anyone curious.
 *
 * Almost all of it the interface communicates by itself. Duration is
 * obvious from a question counter. What you receive is obvious when you
 * receive it. What remains here is the two things that genuinely
 * prevent a misunderstanding, one of which is load-bearing legally:
 * nothing is assessed, and no contact details are needed.
 */
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="label reveal">Intervention Readiness Snapshot</span>
    <h1 class="h1 reveal">Can you stop it in time?</h1>
    <p class="lede muted reveal">
      You have governance. Controls. Named owners.
      Ten questions about one system that runs without you,
      and the last one is the one that matters.
    </p>
    <p class="orientation reveal">
      Intervene measures Intervention Readiness: whether an organisation could
      stop a system it is running before harm becomes irreversible. This takes
      about two minutes, and you can read the whole result without giving a
      name, an email address or an organisation.
    </p>
    <p class="demo-notice reveal">
      <strong>Demonstration only. Withdrawn from use.</strong>
      The result this returns is a worked example. It is the same for every
      visitor and is not read from the answers you give, so it cannot tell you
      anything about your own organisation. The questions are here because the
      framing is worth seeing. The result is not a finding, and Intervene is
      not offering it as one until the result logic is built.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row reveal">
      <a class="btn" href="#snapshot" data-snapshot-begin>Begin</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="/sample-report/">See a sample verdict</a>
    </div>
    <p class="framing reveal">
      No sign-in and no contact details. Nothing here is assessed: it records
      what you tell it. Your answers stay in this tab until you press Generate.
      <a href="/legal/privacy/" class="link">Privacy</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 144px;
    padding-bottom: 128px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
  }
  .hero .label { margin-bottom: 28px; }
  .hero .h1 { max-width: 16ch; }
  .hero .lede { margin-top: 32px; max-width: 56ch; }

  /* For someone who arrived on a forwarded link and has not read a
     single other page. The lede above assumes the site; this does not.
     Quieter than the lede, louder than the framing note below the CTAs,
     because it answers what this is and what it costs to find out. */
  .orientation {
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 48px;
  }

  .framing {
    margin: 56px 0 0;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--faint);
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .hero { padding-top: 112px; padding-bottom: 88px; }
  }

  .demo-notice {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    padding: 16px 0 0 18px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.62;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 56ch;
  }
  .demo-notice strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }
</style>
